{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>A land in which you shall eat bread without poverty.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Ta'anit 4a): 'A land whose stones are iron' — do not read אֲבָנֶיהָ (her stones) but בּוֹנֶיהָ (her builders); these are the Torah scholars who engage in the building of the world, and they sharpen one another like iron. And what did they see to warrant interpreting this verse about Torah learners, in a place where it could be explained according to its plain sense? It appears that the difficulty for them was that this verse begins with matters of eating bread, and afterward says 'and you shall eat and be sated' — so how could Scripture put a gap between things that should be joined, interrupting with [the matter of] iron? Rather, it must certainly be that the entire passage speaks of the conduct of Torah learners, for such is the way of Torah: 'eat bread with salt' (Avot 6:4). And the explanation is that this land — not on account of poverty and destitution shall you eat bread in it as it is, for 'you shall not lack anything in it'; rather, because it is 'a land whose stones' — her builders — 'are iron,' sharpening one another in halakhah, and they will have such desire for Torah that they will not seek any place of sacrificial offerings, to slaughter sheep and cattle, but will be satisfied with bread alone. And do not say that on this account their strength will fail — on the contrary, they will be strong as iron, for the Torah will grant them its strength. And as for what they said, 'אֲבָנֶיהָ — her builders,' there is no need for this, for it is the way of Scripture to designate men of renown by the term 'stones,' as in: 'אֶבֶן מָאֲסוּ הַבּוֹנִים' (Psalms 118:22), 'כִּי אַבְנֵי נֵזֶר מִתְנוֹסְסוֹת' (Zechariah 9:16), 'הָאֶבֶן הָרֹאשָׁה' (ibid. 4:7), and many others. And concerning them it says: 'and you shall eat and be sated,' for the righteous eats to satiate his soul — whether he eats little or much. And likewise what is written 'and from its mountains' — these are the men of renown who are called by the name 'mountains,' as it is stated (Micah 6:2): 'Hear, O mountains, the dispute of YHWH'; from them 'you shall hew copper,' for their bite is the bite of a snake (Avot 2:15). And the expression fits the expression [וּמֵהֲרָרֶיהָ — תַּחְצֹב נְחֹשֶׁת, playing on נָחָשׁ, snake]." "And in the immediately following verse He warns against superfluities and says, 'Lest you eat and be sated' — and He should have said 'lest you eat and become sated' (פֶּן תֹּאכַל וְתִשְׂבַּע)! Rather, what He means is: lest you pursue the superfluous and eat after you are already sated, and build fine houses even though you have already settled — though you already have a dwelling sufficient for your needs — and not be satisfied with what is necessary, but build yet more without need. And on this path we may say that in this very verse as well He mentioned eating and houses: for He said, 'A land in which you shall eat bread without poverty' — that is, the necessary provision in eating, as explained. 'A land whose stones' — that is, her builders — 'are iron,' for one who builds according to his need, that is the strong building like iron, which endures forever. Not so those who build wastefully, for a building of superfluities will certainly become a ruin and desolate, with no inhabitant — as is the way of the courtyards of most princes, in which is fulfilled 'וּשְׁאִיָּה יֻכַּת שָׁעַר' (Isaiah 24:12)." "Another interpretation: מִסְכֵּנֻת — in the sense of storehouses (אוֹצָרוֹת), from the language of 'וַיִּבֶן עָרֵי מִסְכְּנוֹת לְפַרְעֹה' (Exodus 1:11) — that they will not need to make storehouses of grain in the land out of fear of the ravages of famine or war, for when they do the will of the blessed El, their heart will be set, trusting in YHWH who guards them and delivers them." "And there are those who say: since it is the way of the poor man to eat bread as it is, but the rich man does not eat it without a relish (לִפְתָּן), therefore it says that the bread of the land of Israel will be so pure and white as snow that even the rich man will eat it as it is, without relish. Therefore it is stated: 'not in poverty (מִסְכֵּנֻת) shall you eat bread in it.'" "And what is stated, 'and you shall bless YHWH your Elohim for the good land' — this hints at the six occurrences of 'אֶרֶץ' that He mentioned, for they are the source of blessings, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 38b): one does not give the cup of blessing except to one who is good of eye, as it is stated (Proverbs 22:9): 'טוֹב עַיִן הוּא יְבוֹרָךְ' — read it as 'יְבָרֵךְ' (he shall bless). And the additional letter vav hints at the source of blessings, for one who gives a coin to a poor person is blessed with six blessings, and so forth (Bava Batra 9a), while the missing vav of Ephron, who was evil of eye, proves the point. Therefore it is precisely one who is good of eye who blesses, and is blessed with an additional vav, with the six blessings alluded to in the six occurrences of 'אֶרֶץ' that He mentioned here. Therefore it is stated: 'and you shall bless … for the land.' And this is a fitting hint."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {4 "The phrase 'six occurrences of ארץ' refers to the six times the word ארץ appears earlier in the passage (Deut. 8:7–9). The letter-play on יְבוֹרָךְ/יְבָרֵךְ and the vav of Ephron (Gen. 23, where his name is sometimes spelled defectively) is rendered literally to preserve the argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:02.980176715Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}